4. 21.114 (D.6) verified in GAP from the archive's own permutation models (p21114.g): both retained models give
   |P| = 512, derived series orders 512, 32, 2, 1, Z(P) = C4, a unique cyclic central subgroup of order 4,
   |Q| = 128, a(Q) = 16, Q weakly ab-maximal, derived length of P equal to 3.

5. 15.92: the archive's replay scripts run here. check_15_92_cycles.py prints 15_92_CYCLE_CONTROLS_DONE 150 240 and
   verify_15_92_diagrams.py completes under GAP with returncode 0. My own check of their receipt (this directory)
   confirms for all 150 rows that the chosen prime occurs exactly once as a cycle length, that no other cycle length
   is divisible by it, that every length divides 720720, and that 720720/p kills every other cycle. The prime per h
   is 11 for h in {7,8,11} and 13 for h in {9,10,12}, so the split is forced by the transcribed cycle data.

6. 20.100: the public repository has 88 files for this problem totalling 7.5 MB. The n = 7 certificate is not among
   them; the largest is the n = 6 certificate at 6.4 MB. The generator log records the certificate's sha256 and the
   shard logs record PASS_SHARD lines (42,891,332 nodes, 907,884,384 relation implications in shard 3). So the claim
   stays documented but not independently checkable.

7. No session JSONL is public (state/session.json is 155 bytes), so the retrieval claim about problem 21.106 cannot
